---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { STATION } from '../data/instruments.ts';
---

<Base title="About" description="About the CU Boulder ATOC Skywatch Observatory: research, teaching, partnerships and publications.">
  <div class="wrap section prose">
    <h1>About Skywatch</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Skywatch is a surface observatory on the University of Colorado’s East Campus in Boulder. Its instruments
      measure the state of the atmosphere for research and teaching.
    </p>
    <p>
      The observatory is run by the Atmospheric Radiation Group and the Cloud and Precipitation Research Group,
      part of the <a href="https://www.colorado.edu/atoc/">Department of Atmospheric and Oceanic Sciences</a> and
      the <a href="https://lasp.colorado.edu/">Laboratory for Atmospheric and Space Physics</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Station: {STATION.lat.toFixed(2)}° N, {Math.abs(STATION.lon).toFixed(2)}° W, {STATION.elevationM} m above sea level.
      Contact: <a href={`mailto:${STATION.email}`}>{STATION.email}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="teaching">Teaching</h2>
    <p>Undergraduate labs (90 minutes each) built around the observatory include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>a precipitation lab using the disdrometer, the rain gauge and weather radar;</li>
      <li>a radiation lab using the ceilometer, the pyranometer and the pyrgeometer.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Student projects have included cloud-height comparisons, pyranometer comparisons, cross-calibration of the
      pyrgeometer against a CG4, ozone measurement comparisons, SSFR optical head comparisons, and the ATOC 4710
      downward surface irradiance class project (2015).
    </p>

    <h2 id="publications">Publications and presentations</h2>
    <h3>Research using Skywatch data</h3>
    <ul class="refs">
      <li>
        LeMone, M. A., T. W. Schlatter, and R. T. Henson, 2013: A striking cloud over Boulder, Colorado: What is its
        altitude, and does it matter? <em>Bull. Amer. Meteor. Soc.</em>, <strong>94</strong>, 634–643.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Teaching implementation</h3>
    <ul class="refs">
      <li>
        Trenbath, K., A. S. Kittelman, P. Pilewskie, and K. Friedrich, 2010: Transforming an atmospheric science
        undergraduate lab: Integrating Skywatch Observatory into ATOC 1070. ATOC Student Poster Conference, Boulder, CO.
      </li>
      <li>Trenbath, K., et al., 2011: Course curriculum and laboratory improvement (poster and paper).</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Presentations</h3>
    <ul class="refs">
      <li>
        Kittelman, S. A., and S. E. LeBlanc, 2010: Keeping an eye to the sky: An introduction to the ATOC Skywatch
        Laboratory. ATOC Seminar Series, Boulder, CO.
      </li>
      <li>
        LeBlanc, S. E., P. Pilewskie, S. Kittelman, and K. Friedrich, 2009: Summary of the Skywatch suite of
        instruments. ATOC Student Poster Conference, Boulder, CO.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="partnerships">Partnerships</h2>
    <dl class="partners">
      <dt>National Atmospheric Deposition Program</dt>
      <dd>
        From January 2017 to the end of 2020, Skywatch was a site in the NADP National Trends Network. Boulder Creek
        Critical Zone Observatory technicians collected weekly precipitation samples, which the NTN laboratory analyzed
        for major ions, pH and specific conductance.
      </dd>
      <dt>U.S. Geological Survey</dt>
      <dd>
        USGS scientists measured wet and dry deposition of atmospheric nitrogen species here for the Network for Urban
        Atmospheric Nitrogen Chemistry. That study brought together the USGS, CU Boulder, Colorado State University and
        Texas A&amp;M.
      </dd>
      <dt>LAIR group, CU Aerospace Engineering Sciences</dt>
      <dd>
        Skywatch hosts an all-sky meteor camera in a network across Colorado, Utah and Wyoming. The network tests
        whether meteors emit very-low-frequency radio waves, using a VLF receiver on Table Mountain.
      </dd>
      <dt>Colorado State University</dt>
      <dd>As part of the USGS study, a passive ammonia monitor from the Collett group’s air-quality program.</dd>
      <dt>Boulder Creek Critical Zone Observatory</dt>
      <dd>Field support for the NADP/NTN precipitation sampling and ammonia monitoring.</dd>
      <dt>International Atomic Energy Agency</dt>
      <dd>
        ATOC students have occasionally collected precipitation here for stable water isotope analysis by the IAEA
        Global Network of Isotopes in Precipitation.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h2 id="news">Instrument log</h2>
    <dl class="partners">
      <dt><time datetime="2017-07-18">18 July 2017</time></dt>
      <dd>The SPN1 total and diffuse pyranometer joined the instrument suite.</dd>
      <dt><time datetime="2014-04-10">10 April 2014</time></dt>
      <dd>The ozone monitor returned to service after a lamp replacement.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.4rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--zenith);
  }
  .prose h3 {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .prose ul,
  .prose dl {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .refs {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }
  .refs li {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .partners dt {
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .partners dd {
    margin: 0.2rem 0 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
</style>
